  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Landscapes of Liberation is already showing clear reader interest, with 616 recorded downloads and open availability through a hybrid-access route. The book also carries evidence of funded scholarly production, including support from the Swiss National Science Foundation, while its citation record remains at an early stage.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Noah Oehri\u2019s Landscapes of Liberation is a scholarly book published by Leuven University Press in 2023 that examines liberation theology in Andean America and the wider tensions between Catholic mission, colonial power, and geopolitical change. The record presents this as a book rooted in a clearly defined research context, with funding support from the Swiss National Science Foundation and an open-access instance available through the publisher\u2019s DOI landing page.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal in the current record is usage: the book has been downloaded 616 times, with monthly activity showing sustained interest rather than a single isolated spike. That pattern suggests the book has found an audience among readers seeking work on liberation theology, colonialism, agrarian society, and related themes in political and social history.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The surrounding evidence is more modest, which is not unusual for a recent book in a specialized field. OpenAlex currently records no citations, so the scholarly reception visible in this dataset is still at an early stage. At the same time, the book\u2019s open availability and the presence of library-holdings and teaching-adoption infrastructure indicate that it is positioned for continued circulation and classroom discovery.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Concept signals in the record place the book at the intersection of liberation theology, colonial critique, geopolitics, agrarian reform, and transformative learning. Taken together, the data portray a work that is already being used, is accessible to readers, and is embedded in a funded research environment, while its longer-term citation impact has yet to develop.</p>",
